# Orrery

## Overview

Orrery is a machine-building puzzle game for the browser. Each challenge
names reagents and products: constellations of celestial motes. The player
engraves sigils on a hex field, places arms over them, and writes each arm a
looping tape of instructions, then sets the machine running. Arms grab,
swing, and carry motes across the field, sigils bind and transmute what
rests on them, and the machine succeeds when it has delivered every product
`CONSTELLATION_TARGET` (`6`) times without two motes ever colliding.

A finished machine is judged three ways at once: what it cost, how many
cycles it ran, and how much of the field it touched. There is no single
answer to a challenge; there are cheap machines, fast machines, and compact
machines, and the game records a player's best of each.

Orrery has its own name, look, and rules, and its assets, branding, and
design are original to it.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. `specs/field.md` gives
the hex geometry, the motes, and the filaments; `specs/parts.md` the arms,
the wheel, track, and placement; `specs/sigils.md` every sigil's footprint
and effect; `specs/instructions.md` the instruction set and the tapes;
`specs/simulation.md` the cycle, motion, collision, faults, and metrics;
`specs/controls.md` the pointer and the keyboard actions; `specs/editor.md`
the machine editor; `specs/formats.md` the challenge and solution documents;
`specs/challenges.md` the fixed Extras challenges; `specs/state.md` what the
game's state carries; `specs/instrumentation.md` the debug and automation
surface; and `specs/ui.md` the screens and audio, with the two modes in
`specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/extras.md`. Read all of them and
build the game they describe as one cohesive whole.

