## The runtime

This game stands on no engine, so the runtime under it is yours to write:
the frame loop and the delta time it measures, the canvas fit, input from
both the keyboard and the pointer, audio cues, and the debug overlay. Where
this specification speaks of the runtime it means that layer, and it states
only what Orrery needs from it. How the layer is structured is yours to
decide.
{{else}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in
the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It owns the frame
loop and the delta time the game is given, the input actions the menus and
editor verbs are driven by, the pointer the editor is operated with, audio
cues, asset loading, the canvas fit, and the debug overlay. Read its
documentation before you start. This specification states only what is
specific to Orrery.

The pointer reaches the game from the engine already in the stage's logical
units, with its press and release edges. `specs/controls.md` states what
Orrery does with them.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested
with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain
is fixed and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`. The directory does not exist yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is
where your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop
and the canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, the diagnostics
overlay, the debug and automation surface, the figures this specification
fixes, and the game itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. `specs/state.md` states what it must
carry, and `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one contract fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and
  `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need,
added along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and
scripts stay as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy
this specification fixes is stated here under a name. Name each one once in
a module of your own and read it from there, rather than restating a value
at each use. Anything this specification does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as
`src/**/*.test.ts`. `npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node,
with coverage measured over `src/`. A test drives the game over its own
clock for a counted number of frames, so it needs no browser, and
`@napi-rs/canvas` is installed for a test that wants a real 2D context.
Cover the parts of the game a person cannot see at a glance: the placement
rules, the tape period and the macro expansions, grab and carry, the torn
rule, collision at the sample fractions, each sigil's condition, set
matching, plain and repeating, and the three metrics.
{{else}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<OrreryState, OrreryDebugApi>`:
`initialize` builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them
together as `[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a
read-only view (`DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`) and returns the next state,
advanced against the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed
that next state, read-only again, and draws it. The engine holds the state
by value and replaces it with what each `update` returns, so a frame builds
the next state from the current one rather than writing into it, and
nothing but `update` and the debug surface's poses advances the game. All
three functions throw `"not implemented"` as they stand. Implement them,
and split the work across new files under `src/` however suits you.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `OrreryState` and the types it is
built from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `OrreryDebugApi`,
the debug and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the sky
itself. The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color
your sky uses.

