### The checks the repository passes

Four commands run over the finished repository before it is reviewed:

| Command | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | The code type-checks. A repository that does not compile is not reviewed. |
| `npx eslint .` | The code is lint-clean. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | The code is formatted. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Your tests pass, with their count and coverage reported. |

They run on the repository as you leave it. `npm run typecheck`,
`npm run lint`, `npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served
as-is at the root of any static file server. The scripts that do this are
already in `package.json`.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a
browser. This is a substantial front-end build: a rules-exact simulation
with simultaneous motion and collision, a direct-manipulation editor with
drags, tapes, and undo, a course of challenges you design yourself, and a
presentation that makes a machine of spinning arms worth watching. Aim for
a game a person would enjoy playing and replaying for better records.

The build ships both of the game's modes, chosen from the title menu:
Campaign, the course of challenges you author to
`specs/modes/campaign.md`, and Extras, the fixed shelf of
`specs/modes/extras.md`. The field, the parts, the simulation, the editor,
and the debug surface the other spec files describe are the same in both.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time,
  the canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input
  actions, the pointer, audio, and the debug overlay from it.
{{/if}}
- Ship both modes, Campaign and Extras, each reachable from the title menu,
  with the Extras challenges exactly as `specs/challenges.md` writes them
  and a campaign of your own that satisfies `specs/modes/campaign.md`,
  reference solutions included.
- Render real graphics on the canvas, drawn as shapes and text rather than
  as an ASCII or text-only rendering, and animate a run's motion smoothly
  along the same paths the collision rule samples.
- Draw everything in code. The build ships no art or audio files and
  fetches nothing at runtime.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is
  self-contained, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or
  play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in one value, the one the frame loop advances
  and the debug surface reads.
{{else}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `OrreryState`, as `specs/state.md`
  declares it.
{{/if}}
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how
  to install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the
  static production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is written as for a long-lived codebase shared with human
developers.

- Modules are split by concern, and each has one responsibility.
- Every fixed figure is named once and imported where it is used.
- Logic appears once; no two places implement the same rule.
- The tree carries no dead code, no commented-out code, and no leftover
  debugging output.
- Comments explain intent where the code cannot.
- The type-check, lint, and format checks pass.
- Unit tests cover the simulation and the editor's rules.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own
logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9).
The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y`
increases downward. The stage center is `(STAGE_CX, STAGE_CY)`
(`640, 360`). Where the field sits within the stage, and where each hex's
center falls, are given in `specs/field.md`; the editor's regions are given
in `specs/editor.md`.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the
  aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The
  complete stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and
  at any pixel density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the
  runtime alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.
  The pointer position the game is given is in those same units, so a hit
  test against a hex center needs no conversion.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in
seconds, integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update. A
run's own unit is the cycle, and `specs/simulation.md` fixes how cycles
advance against that delta time.

## Visual design

The sky is dark and the machine is the show. Choose the colors and the
typography, decide how a mote, a filament, an arm, and each sigil's
engraving are drawn, and decide how a running machine moves and glows.

Legibility is gameplay, so whatever look you choose, a player reads
everything the legibility lists fix: the mote classes and types in
`specs/field.md`, the parts and their working state in `specs/parts.md`,
the editor's selection, ghosts, cursor, and tape glyphs in
`specs/editor.md`, and every piece of text on the stage. The palette, type,
glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
